From: conda-forge onnxruntime-feedstock
Subject: [PATCH] Optionally split libonnxruntime into an internal and a public library

The Python bindings link the static libraries, so they contain a second copy
of everything in libonnxruntime, with its own global state.

The new option onnxruntime_BUILD_INTERNAL_SHARED_LIB builds the
implementation as onnxruntime_internal, which the Python bindings link ahead
of the static libraries, so that the linker takes only what
onnxruntime_internal lacks from them. libonnxruntime keeps exporting only
the public API, which it forwards to onnxruntime_internal:

- Linux: libonnxruntime_internal.so.<version> exports every symbol under
  ORT_INTERNAL_<version> except the public API and the vendored cpuinfo
  symbols. Each public function of libonnxruntime.so.1 is a GNU indirect
  function that resolves to the implementation through
  GetFunctionEntryByName.
- macOS: libonnxruntime_internal.<version>.dylib is built with default
  visibility and exports everything but the vendored cpuinfo symbols.
  libonnxruntime.1.dylib re-exports the public API from it with
  -reexported_symbols_list.
- Windows (with the Python bindings): a DLL cannot export everything, so
  gen_internal_def.py runs dumpbin over the objects of the Python bindings
  and exports the public API plus every symbol they take from the static
  libraries, except the protobuf classes of onnx. The bindings import the
  data they use with __declspec(dllimport) (ORT_IMPORT_INTERNAL_DATA) and
  rely on the DllMain of onnxruntime_internal.dll. The exports are only
  rewritten when they change, so rebuilding the bindings does not relink
  the DLL. onnxruntime.dll forwards the public API in its module-definition
  file.
---
